        "slide": 9,
        "role": "close",
        "subject": "A larger open course binder in tangerine bookcloth on a cobalt sweep. It has an empty rubric grid, a greeked outline, chrome three-ring hardware, five pastel tabs and a blue pen. Two closed satin laptops are stacked up and to the right, each with a cyan sleep light.",
        "visual_consequence": "Without the headline, an empty rubric and two shut laptops read as a curriculum not yet decided. My last revise landed. The hard royal-blue offset blocks are gone. The binder now rests on the sweep: a soft indigo occlusion is darkest right at its cover edge and fades outward, deepest along the near and left edges (slide-09-r2c1, cover edge from tile x about 210 to 400; slide-09-r3c1, near edge at tile y about 40 to 160). The laptop stack has the same soft indigo contact under its near and right edges (slide-09-r2c2, tile y about 300 to 360). The tabs now sit in that same soft shade instead of on hard blue blocks. The group fills about half the frame and the empty upper-left band has closed. The binder is fully inside the frame, the page edges are plain paper, and the pen keeps its shadow.",
        "weakness": "Minor. The occlusion was built as a large rectangular soft plane, and its straight outer boundaries faintly show on the sweep. One soft straight edge runs diagonally across the upper left (slide-09-r2c1, from tile x 0, y about 100 to x 1080, y about 190), and a near-vertical lighter step sits right of the tabs (slide-09-r2c2, tile x about 730 to 770, y 280 to 900). They read as a faint panel rather than a seam or a muddy band, and they are barely visible at 432 px. The halo also wraps every side of the binder almost evenly, so the lean away from the key is weak.",
